<p>Quantum computers promise dramatic speedups for some problems, but
quantum programs are hard to get right: their behavior is
probabilistic, their state cannot be inspected without disturbing it,
and the hardware is scarce and noisy.  I have explored how programming
languages and formal verification can help, developing verified
compilers and optimizers for quantum circuits, a formally certified
implementation of Shor's factoring algorithm, and languages that unify
quantum and classical computation.
</p>
